<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindu-Buddhist</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hindu-Buddhist refers to the blended religious and cultural tradition, drawing on both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, that shaped many states of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> was its last great expression in the Indonesian archipelago. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread through the region, Hindu-Buddhist culture retreated, surviving most notably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1408 a king of Po-ni with a <b>Hindu-Buddhist</b> name, Maharaja Karna, travelled to the Ming capital at Nanjing and died there, and some historians therefore place the court's conversion to Islam in the fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Majapahit's government was centred on the king, whose authority was reinforced by <b>Hindu-Buddhist</b> ideals of divine kingship that presented him as an incarnation of the gods and an intermediary between the human and spiritual realms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Malacca was governed by written law, the Undang-Undang Melaka, also called the Hukum Kanun Melaka, and the Maritime Laws, shaped by <b>Hindu-Buddhist</b> tradition, Islam and indigenous adat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion combined Islam, adopted by the Javanese courts during the previous centuries, with older Javanese and <b>Hindu-Buddhist</b> traditions: rulers claimed the Islamic titles of sultan or susuhunan and also a mystical bond with Ratu Kidul, the spirit queen of the southern ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Medang Kingdom, the Mataram of its inscriptions, was the <b>Hindu-Buddhist</b> kingdom of Central and later East Java from Sanjaya's lingga of 732 to the pralaya of 1016–1017, whose Shailendra kings built Borobudur and Prambanan, raided Champa and Cambodia and ruled Srivijaya, and whose last king Dharmawangsa died…</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslim merchants and teachers established communities in the ports, and once Cirebon had broken away and Demak had become the leading power of central Java, the <b>Hindu-Buddhist</b> kingdom lost control of the harbours that provided its trade revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
